# Deployment & Infrastructure — GitHub → Railway or AWS

## Principle (both platforms)
**GitHub first, infrastructure second.** Code and infrastructure reach any environment only by merging on GitHub;
the merge triggers the deploy. Nobody deploys from a laptop or a cloud console. The chosen platform is recorded in
ADR-0001 and set in the GitHub variable `HOSTING` (`railway` | `aws`), which `pipeline.yml` reads.

## Environments (both platforms)
| Environment | Branch | Deploy trigger |
|---|---|---|
| `development` | `develop` | merge to `develop`, after verification |
| `staging` | `staging` (and `hotfix/*` for verification) | merge to `staging`, after verification |
| `production` | `main` | merge to `main`, after verification **and** GitHub Environment approval |

## One service per component
| Component | Railway | AWS |
|---|---|---|
| web (site + admin) | service `web` | ECS Fargate service `<prefix>-web` behind ALB |
| landing (if separate) | service `landing` | ECS service `<prefix>-landing` (or S3 + CloudFront if static) |
| server engine | service `engine` | ECS service `<prefix>-engine` |
| worker | service `worker` | ECS service `<prefix>-worker` (same image as engine, different command) |
| agents | service `agents` | ECS service `<prefix>-agents` |
| PostgreSQL | Railway Postgres | RDS PostgreSQL (Multi-AZ in production) |
| Redis | Railway Redis | ElastiCache Redis / Valkey |

## Railway (default)
- One project per product; environments as above. `pipeline.yml` deploys with a Railway project token per GitHub Environment; Railway autodeploy **off**.
- Reference variables between services, private networking, pre-deploy migrations, `railway.json` per service when used. Skill: `deploy-railway`.

## AWS (when chosen)
- **Infrastructure as Code only** (Terraform default, or CDK) in `infra/`, one stack/workspace per environment. Changes go
  through PRs: `infra-aws.yml` runs `terraform plan` on the PR and `apply` after merge, with production approval.
- `pipeline.yml` (HOSTING=aws): GitHub OIDC role per environment (no access keys) → build and push images to ECR (tag = commit SHA)
  → migrations as a one-off ECS task → new task-definition revisions → `update-service` → wait until stable.
- Naming conventions the pipeline relies on: ECS service `<ECS_SERVICE_PREFIX>-<svc>`, container name `<svc>`, ECR repo
  `<ECR_REPO_PREFIX>-<svc>`, Dockerfile `apps/<svc>/Dockerfile`.
- ECS deployment circuit breaker with automatic rollback; secrets from Secrets Manager/SSM injected into task definitions.
- Region `il-central-1` (Tel Aviv) when data residency/latency matters. Tags `client`, `project`, `env`, `owner=spacode`. AWS Budgets alerts.
- Skill: `deploy-aws`.

## Containers / builds (both)
Multi-stage Dockerfile, non-root, pinned LTS base, configuration only via env vars, healthcheck `/health/ready` on every
HTTP service. Apps serve `/version.json` (written by the pipeline with the commit SHA) so the pipeline can confirm the new
version is live before running post-deploy tests.

## Mobile apps (Expo)
Built and shipped by **EAS** from GitHub workflows — see `12-mobile-expo`. Store submission requires human approval.

## Domains
`dev.<domain>`, `staging.<domain>` (protected, `noindex`), `<domain>` / `www` / `app.`; API at `api.` per environment.

## Monitoring & rollback
- Sentry per environment, uptime checks on `/health/ready`, alerts; Railway usage alerts or CloudWatch alarms + AWS Budgets.
- Rollback = revert the merge on GitHub (preferred). Emergency: redeploy the previous Railway deployment / previous ECS
  task-definition revision, then reconcile Git the same day. DB changes must stay compatible with N-1 code.
- Watch errors for 30 minutes after every production deploy.
